<section xml:id="sec-ch04-05-ChangingOrderOfIntegration" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Changing Order of Integration</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      As with double integrals over rectangular regions, under some
      continuity assumptions, we may change the order of integration.
    </p>
  </note>

  <theorem>
    <title>Fubini's Theorem (General Regions)</title>

    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>f(x, y)</m> be continuous on a region <m>R</m>.</p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>R</m> is defined by
              <m>a \leq x \leq b, g_{1}(x) \leq y \leq h_{1}(x)</m>, with
              <m>g_{1}</m> and <m>h_{1}</m> continuous on <m>[a, b]</m>, then
              <md>
                \iint_{R} f(x, y) d A=\int_{a}^{b} \int_{g_{1}(x)}^{h_{1}(x)} f(x, y) d y d x
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>R</m> is defined by
              <m>c \leq y \leq d, g_{2}(y) \leq y \leq h_{2}(y)</m>, with
              <m>g_{2}</m> and <m>h_{2}</m> continuous on <m>[c, d]</m>, then
              <md>
                \iint_{R} f(x, y) d A=\int_{c}^{d} \int_{g_{2}(y)}^{h_{2}(y)} f(x, y) d x d y
              </md>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Changing the order of integration over regions that are not
      rectangular requires more work. An easy way to do it is by drawing a
      sketch of the region of integration.
    </p>

    <p>
      This means that we should be able to draw the region of integration
      just by looking at the bounds of integration from the iterated
      integrals.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-05-ex-18-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the integral
        <m>\int_{-1}^{1} \int_{ x^{2}}^{2- x^2} 2xy d y d x</m> :
      </p>

      <p>(a) Draw a sketch of the region of integration.</p>

      <p>(b) Use your sketch to change the order of integration.</p>

      <p>(c) Evaluate the double integral.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The bounds <m>-1\leq x \leq 1</m> and <m>x^2\leq y \leq 2-x^2</m>
        describe the region between the upward-opening parabola
        <m>y=x^2</m> and the downward-opening parabola <m>y=2-x^2</m>. They
        intersect when
        <m>x^2=2-x^2 \Rightarrow x^2=1 \Rightarrow x=\pm 1</m>, confirming
        the outer bounds. The region lies between the two parabolas for
        <m>-1\leq x\leq 1</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-05-ex-18-1">
        <caption>The domain of integration, the area between the parabolas</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Example 18.1. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-05-ex-18-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Example-18.1.png">
              <shortdescription>The domain of integration, the area between the parabolas</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The domain of integration, the area between the parabolas</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
      <!--\includegraphics[max width=0.5\textwidth, center]{2025_10_25_a794e0d5148c83ceb0d0g-2}-->
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        To integrate in the order <m>dx\,dy</m>, we need to express
        <m>x</m> as a function of <m>y</m>. From the two parabolas:
        <m>y=x^2</m> gives <m>x=\pm\sqrt{y}</m>, and <m>y=2-x^2</m> gives
        <m>x=\pm\sqrt{2-y}</m>. The region has <m>y</m> ranging from the
        bottom of the lower parabola to the top of the upper one. The lower
        parabola has its vertex at <m>(0,0)</m> and the upper at
        <m>(0,2)</m>; they meet at <m>y=1</m>. For <m>0\leq y\leq 1</m> the
        horizontal cross-section is bounded on the left and right by
        <m>x=-\sqrt{y}</m> and <m>x=\sqrt{y}</m>. For <m>1\leq y\leq 2</m>
        the cross-section is bounded by <m>x=-\sqrt{2-y}</m> and
        <m>x=\sqrt{2-y}</m>. Hence, the integral with reversed order is
        <md>
          \int_0^1\int_{-\sqrt{y}}^{\sqrt{y}} 2xy\, dx\, dy + \int_1^2\int_{-\sqrt{2-y}}^{\sqrt{2-y}} 2xy\, dx\, dy
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Evaluating using the original order is more straightforward here.
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_{-1}^{1} \int_{x^{2}}^{2-x^2} 2xy\, dy\, dx = \int_{-1}^{1} \left. xy^2 \right|_{x^2}^{2-x^2} dx = \int_{-1}^{1} x\left[(2-x^2)^2 - x^4\right] dx</mrow>
          <mrow>= \int_{-1}^{1} x\left[4 - 4x^2 + x^4 - x^4\right] dx = \int_{-1}^{1} x(4-4x^2)\, dx</mrow>
          <mrow>= \int_{-1}^{1} 4x - 4x^3\, dx = \left. 2x^2 - x^4 \right|_{-1}^{1} = (2-1)-(2-1) = 0</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-05-ex-18-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the integral
        <m>\int_{-1}^{0} \int_{x}^{-x^2} 2x+2y\, d y\, d x</m> :
      </p>

      <p>(a) Draw a sketch of the region of integration.</p>

      <p>(b) Use your sketch to change the order of integration.</p>

      <p>(c) Evaluate the double integral.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The bounds <m>-1\leq x\leq 0</m> and <m>x\leq y\leq -x^2</m>
        describe the region above the line <m>y=x</m> and below the
        downward-opening parabola <m>y=-x^2</m>. They intersect when
        <m>x=-x^2 \Rightarrow x^2+x=0 \Rightarrow x(x+1)=0</m>, giving
        <m>x=0</m> and <m>x=-1</m>, confirming the outer bounds. For
        <m>-1\leq x\leq 0</m> the line lies below the parabola.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-05-ex-18-2">
        <caption>The domain of integration, the area between the parabola and the line</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Example 18.2. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-05-ex-18-2" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Example-18.2.png">
              <shortdescription>The domain of integration, the area between the parabola and the line</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The domain of integration, the area between the parabola and the line</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
      <!--\includegraphics[max width=0.5\textwidth, center]{2025_10_25_a794e0d5148c83ceb0d0g-3}-->
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        To integrate in the order <m>dx\,dy</m>, we express <m>x</m> as a
        function of <m>y</m>. From <m>y=-x^2</m> we get <m>x=-\sqrt{-y}</m>
        (taking the negative square root since <m>x\leq 0</m>), so the
        parabola gives the left boundary. From <m>y=x</m> we get
        <m>x=y</m>, which gives the right boundary. The variable <m>y</m>
        ranges from the bottom intersection point to the top. At
        <m>(-1,-1)</m> both curves meet, giving <m>y=-1</m> as the lower
        bound. The parabola reaches <m>y=0</m> at <m>x=0</m>, and the line
        also passes through <m>(0,0)</m>, giving <m>y=0</m> as the upper
        bound. Hence the reversed integral is
        <md>
          \int_{-1}^{0}\int_{-\sqrt{-y}}^{y} 2x+2y\, dx\, dy
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Evaluating using the original order is more straightforward here.
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\int_{-1}^{0}\int_{x}^{-x^2} 2x+2y\, dy\, dx = \int_{-1}^{0} \left. 2xy + y^2 \right|_{x}^{-x^2} dx</mrow>
          <mrow>= \int_{-1}^{0} \left[2x(-x^2)+(-x^2)^2\right] - \left[2x(x)+x^2\right] dx</mrow>
          <mrow>= \int_{-1}^{0} \left[-2x^3 + x^4\right] - \left[2x^2+x^2\right] dx</mrow>
          <mrow>= \int_{-1}^{0} x^4 - 2x^3 - 3x^2\, dx</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left. \frac{x^5}{5} - \frac{x^4}{2} - x^3 \right|_{-1}^{0} = 0 - \left(-\frac{1}{5} - \frac{1}{2} + 1\right) = \frac{1}{5}+\frac{1}{2}-1 = -\frac{3}{10}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Volume Between Surfaces and Area</title>

    <p>
      Just like in Calculus II we were able to find area bounded between
      functions, we can similarly find the volume bounded between surfaces.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Volume Between Surfaces</title>

    <p>
      Suppose that <m>f(x, y)</m> and <m>g(x, y)</m> are continuous on a
      region <m>R</m> and that <m>f(x, y) \geq g(x, y)</m> on <m>R</m>. Then
      the volume bounded by <m>f(x, y)</m> and <m>g(x, y)</m> on <m>R</m> is
      given by
      <md>
        V=\iint_{R}(f(x, y)-g(x, y)) d A
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-05-ex-18-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) an iterated integral that gives the
        volume of the solid in the first octant ( <m>x, y, z \geq 0</m> )
        bounded by the surfaces <m>z=xy+5</m>, <m>z=2x+2y</m> above the
        circle <m>x^{2}+y^2=1</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-05-ex-18-3">
        <caption>The surface z equals x times y plus 5, the plane 2 x plus 2 y, and the area above the unit circle in the first octant between the surface and the plane.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_25_a794e0d5148c83ceb0d0g-4. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-05-ex-18-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_25_a794e0d5148c83ceb0d0g-4.png">
              <shortdescription>The surface z equals x times y plus 5, the plane 2 x plus 2 y, and the area above the unit circle in the first octant between the surface and the plane.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The surface <m>z=xy+5</m>, the plane <m>2x+2y</m>, and the area above the unit circle in the first octant between the surface and the plane.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The volume bounded by the given surfaces
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        First we determine which surface is on top. The region of
        integration <m>R</m> is the part of the unit disk
        <m>x^2+y^2\leq 1</m> in the first quadrant, i.e.
        <m>1\geq x\geq 0</m> and <m>1\geq y\geq 0</m>. This implies that
        <m> \geq xy +5 \geq 5 \gt 4 \geq 2x+2y</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        For a fixed <m>x\in[0,1]</m>, <m>y</m> ranges from <m>0</m> to
        <m>\sqrt{1-x^2}</m>. Hence the volume is
        <md>
          V = \int_0^1\int_0^{\sqrt{1-x^2}} \left[(xy+5)-(2x+2y)\right] dy\, dx = \int_0^1\int_0^{\sqrt{1-x^2}} xy - 2x - 2y + 5\, dy\, dx
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Finding Area Using Double Integrals</title>

    <p>
      Suppose you have a region <m>R</m> in the <m>x y</m>-plane, you can
      find the area of <m>R</m> by using the double integral
      <md>
        \iint_{R} d A
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      That is, integrating the function <m>f(x, y)=1</m> over <m>R</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-05-ex-18-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Explain geometrically why the area of <m>R</m> is given by
        <m>\iint_{R} d A</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Recall that <m>\iint_R f(x,y)\, dA</m> gives the net volume of the
        solid bounded by the surface <m>z=f(x,y)</m> and the
        <m>xy</m>-plane, over the region <m>R</m>. Choosing
        <m>f(x,y)=1</m> means the surface is the horizontal plane
        <m>z=1</m>, which sits exactly one unit above the <m>xy</m>-plane at
        every point of <m>R</m>. The solid bounded between <m>z=0</m> and
        <m>z=1</m> over <m>R</m> is therefore a slab of uniform height 1.
        Its volume equals base area times height, that is
        <m>\text{Area}(R)\times 1 = \text{Area}(R)</m>. Hence
        <md>
          \iint_R 1\, dA = \text{Area}(R)
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
